-- title:
--   Small Fourier coefficients force pole-chart values into the maximal ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and $\ell$ a prime with $A$-valuation of $\ell$ less than $1$ (so $\ell$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$) and with $\ell \nmid M$. Write $F$ for the subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ obtained by adjoining to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ the coefficientwise images under `coeffEmb` of the field $\mathbb{Q}(\,q\text{-expansions } \mathrm{qExpand}\,\mathbb{Q}\,d\,j_q : d \mid M)$, and let $w$ be a `Place` of $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$: a proper valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and a principal ideal ring. Assume there is $a_0 \in A$ with $w.\mathrm{ord}$ of $\bar j^{-1} - a_0$ strictly positive, where $\bar j \in F$ is the coefficientwise image of $j_q$; thus $1/j$ has value $a_0$ at $w$. Let $b \in F$ be a root of some monic polynomial with coefficients in the subring generated by the image of $A$ together with $\bar j^{-1}$, and suppose every Laurent coefficient $b_n$ of $b$ has $A$-valuation less than $1$. If, for some $a \in A$, the difference $b - a$ is a non-unit of the valuation ring of $w$, then the $A$-valuation of $a$ is less than $1$.
--
--   This is a pole-chart form of the $q$-expansion principle modulo the maximal ideal of $A$: a function integral over the chart ring $A[1/j]$ whose Fourier coefficients all lie in the maximal ideal takes values in the maximal ideal at each place of the pole chart where it has a value. It is used in the analysis of the special fibre of the fibre model of the modular curve of level prime to the residue characteristic, in particular in the Frobenius and inertia computations at the places attached to the cusp chart.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_valuation_lt_one_of_sub_mem_nonunits_of_coeff_lt_one_inf.lean

